#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "53ce2176-ae44-578d-8fba-35c5018d4162" :question/math-academy-id "e-5075" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3,\n$$\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "b322617c-ab21-5ce8-997f-2f10813b4280" :question/math-academy-id "e-5098" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n\n$$\nx=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6).$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3.$ This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073429/assets/f15f25af0c4804dc811a0ab231ba2ef3ecce2ecd12501a1f2d8b9e34b71a505b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a792fa16-e1fd-5f30-a128-bbc49a285429" :question/math-academy-id "e-5099" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty , \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={t}^{2}-6t \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-(3)^{2} \\\\  & ={(t-3)}^{2}-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left( t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0,$ we can conclude that $x \\geq -9.$\n\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4,$ notice that $t^4 \\geq 0,$ and consequently $y \\geq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x \\geq -9$ and $y \\geq 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087477 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "25ab094c-a35e-5507-a6df-459361ff6fcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5292" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos\\theta, \\,\\,\\, y = 2\\sin\\theta, \\quad 0\\leq \\theta <     2\\pi,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\cos{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -4 & \\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 4 \\\\ -4 & \\le x\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\leq\\theta <  2\\pi,$ we have $-1 \\leq \\sin{\\theta} \\leq 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-4 \\leq x \\leq 4$ and $-2 \\leq y \\leq 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c51b6d1c-4b3d-5117-83bc-1da55205730f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381279} {:answer/id #uuid "2748f020-36e7-5550-8ffa-ffc7d962735b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381280} {:answer/id #uuid "26aca4a4-b1a0-5e8a-9497-de009ec98840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381281} {:answer/id #uuid "8513efe0-ddb5-5268-a39f-3ee7c12f2ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381282} {:answer/id #uuid "a7addb19-9633-57f5-93c9-35339a4b39ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8513efe0-ddb5-5268-a39f-3ee7c12f2ecb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381282} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad618d1-235b-5925-afe2-0184fde51cd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64b5f2bd-0930-55dd-b208-e74ffa77b011" :question/math-academy-id "q-123763" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\sqrt{t+9},\\,\\,\\,y=t+1,\\,t\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nwhat is the range of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the range of $x,$ note that $\\sqrt{t+9}\\ge 3$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Consequently, $x=2\\sqrt{t+9}\\ge 6.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that $x\\ge 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf36275d-b1ce-5fa5-baac-0c8f5a6f9b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le -3,\\,y\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381286} {:answer/id #uuid "6a9d2a05-16ca-5a5a-8adc-be64a53fb485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge -3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381287} {:answer/id #uuid "d469f98d-b407-5209-a1c7-c69ae82764ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381288} {:answer/id #uuid "32e1787a-9224-57f6-aac6-3f2e9582bb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge -1,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381289} {:answer/id #uuid "6d462a95-0202-5902-85b9-5c8e6d47059e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 3,\\,y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d469f98d-b407-5209-a1c7-c69ae82764ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\le 2,\\,y\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381288} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86aca44-c7c0-5583-9c31-3cd0b8f79e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3e9c6f6-566c-5162-9fe9-4d86fd16d1a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123769" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3-t,\\,\\,\\,y=t+2,\\,t\\ge 1,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $3-t\\le 2$ for all $t\\ge 1.$ Consequently, $x=3-t\\le 2.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $t+2\\ge 3$ for all $t\\ge 1.$ Consequently, $y=t+2\\ge 3.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $x\\le 2$ and $y\\ge 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a068b4-beba-59d2-9684-8b9d0a93f30d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-4\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381293} {:answer/id #uuid "51b87ecd-07e7-57e5-a1e7-aa8798e8043c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381294} {:answer/id #uuid "d4339d45-466e-5991-8fb6-1fbda2b7b039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,-3\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381295} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cef507-d962-52ca-bf5c-54891e193c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381296} {:answer/id #uuid "87081915-cabe-50f0-ab0e-9cdfce979ecc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cef507-d962-52ca-bf5c-54891e193c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,2\\le y\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381296} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74e8d49-0a7c-5237-b08b-b742646e39bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df6cb91b-a536-5526-9dd6-80b470cc8a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-123775" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+3 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +3\\le 1+3 \\\\ 2 & \\le y\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $2\\le y\\le 4.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$ and $2\\le y\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/answer