bstitute $t=5$ into $x={t}^{3}-2t.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(5)=(5)^{3}-2(5)=125-10=115.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $21$ | $56$ | $115$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ | $-\\frac{13}{2}$ | $-\\frac{17}{2}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381354 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd11f182-0e1e-5c9a-bdf5-5ac144f72f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd11f182-0e1e-5c9a-bdf5-5ac144f72f2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381356} :answer-field/id #uuid "362a972b-776c-5d19-a657-42691609e511" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381355} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd58d37a-8421-5f00-81df-170f502684dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd58d37a-8421-5f00-81df-170f502684dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381358} :answer-field/id #uuid "b13b322a-8d9f-5299-91d1-a9b26cc406c6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381357} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f57b8d1d-e730-5c60-9ed1-7df680ce5409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f57b8d1d-e730-5c60-9ed1-7df680ce5409" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "92" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381360} :answer-field/id #uuid "8df307dc-6355-5406-b541-c5059bc1d6b2" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbb863b6-5061-593d-ac3e-c0571c45f351" :question/math-academy-id "q-326247" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-2t+2,\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12,\\,-1\\le t\\le 4.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $5$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $12$ | $11$ | {{field-2}} | $39$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(1),$ we substitute $t=1$ into $x={t}^{2}-2t+2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(1)=(1)^{2}-2(1)+2=1-2+2=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+12=16-12+12=16.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(4),$ we substitute $t=4$ into $y=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+12.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(4)=2(4)^{3}-3(4)^{2}+12=128-48+12=92.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n| $y$ | $7$ | $12$ | $11$ | $16$ | $39$ | $92$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97173aa2-a18f-53d2-8c98-d425fd1fefaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381363} {:answer/id #uuid "151846e9-97ee-5ed8-b7a6-748da92d3039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "151846e9-97ee-5ed8-b7a6-748da92d3039" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381364} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae60b0c-ffb6-5c11-b7ad-9c8ec1e5cef1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82b31578-c0bb-5d14-a969-490c0c720d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82b31578-c0bb-5d14-a969-490c0c720d29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381366} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c4be21a-c35b-5450-927c-88f3811f4612" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381365} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "035b34e7-fb3e-5f22-911b-9e2a895975f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "035b34e7-fb3e-5f22-911b-9e2a895975f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381368} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0efe6ea-d20a-53f0-8f3d-56b5252f6df1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a97c8a7-4206-5317-aa26-66c1084934bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-326250" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}-2,\\,y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1},\\,0\\le t\\le 5.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{23}{2}$ | $30$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | {{field-2}} | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(5),$ we substitute $t=5$ into $x=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3}-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(5)=\\frac{1}{2}(5)^{3}-2=\\frac{125}{2}-\\frac{4}{2}=\\frac{121}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=\\frac{2-1}{2+1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(5),$ we substitute $t=5$ into $y=\\frac{t-1}{t+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(0)=\\frac{5-1}{5+1}=\\frac{4}{6}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{23}{2}$ | $30$ | $\\frac{121}{2}$ |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{5}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301646 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064dada6-813e-5fa4-8e1b-1c9a28bc69a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301648} {:answer/id #uuid "68fd86af-d7cc-584f-a636-fbd90ff0f0de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "064dada6-813e-5fa4-8e1b-1c9a28bc69a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301648} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bc03e44-c691-5927-b19c-cefe4658eefe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301647} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b2a9454b-3567-54e1-824c-9a76f9e00dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a9454b-3567-54e1-824c-9a76f9e00dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301650} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fb061e-80e8-540a-87a4-ed021b4b7a69" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301649} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cfb2a80-fa63-5a5c-8b2b-eaad187b6a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cfb2a80-fa63-5a5c-8b2b-eaad187b6a42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301652} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede85813-e8f1-5d08-9ccb-18f26453d2af" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6aebe50f-dc47-519c-b6ab-6e3d67578350" :question/math-academy-id "q-326251" :question/problem "Consider the curve with parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t,\\,-3\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | {{field-1}} | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | {{field-2}} | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $x(-3),$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $x=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx(-3)=\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(-1),$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}(-1)^{2}-(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}+1=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $y(2),$ we substitute $t=2$ into $y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}-t.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny(2)=\\frac{3}{2}(2)^{2}-(2)=6-2=4.\n$$\n\nThen the table is \n\n \n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ |\n| $y$ | $\\frac{33}{2}$ | $8$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $4$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fccdb3a-12ba-5e2c-8d64-6f9794c5bdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fccdb3a-12ba-5e2c-8d64-6f9794c5bdbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381372} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d565cf4-11d3-5f68-934d-ffaaec3a6e9f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381371} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a32644c-48c3-5138-ad52-1b4f273800aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a32644c-48c3-5138-ad52-1b4f273800aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381374} :answer-field/id #uuid "5427032e-aafe-5cd2-8475-4bfa795898bb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2453938b-9c5b-5f9b-a21c-da710df8621e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2453938b-9c5b-5f9b-a21c-da710df8621e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381376} :answer-field/id #uuid "17f45565-d11c-5847-92e7-14fe5be9fc4e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b448269e-3ed1-50ce-84a7-078cc60de477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b448269e-3ed1-50ce-84a7-078cc60de477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381378} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1fdc665-4e94-5f79-93ea-20a7ce30a6a7" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3aede82d-ef76-5a4b-99e8-bfc21ba05408" :question/math-academy-id "q-327660" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-5,\\,y=\\sqrt{\\,t+4\\,}+1,\\,t\\ge -4,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}$,\\,y$\n{{field-3}}{{field-4}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ note that $t-5\\ge -9$ for all $t\\ge -4.$ Consequently, the range of $x$ is\n\n$$\nx\\ge -9.\n$$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ note that $\\sqrt{t+4}\\ge 0$ for all $t\\ge -4.$ Consequently, the range of $y$ is\n\n$$\ny\\ge 1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca2fba3f-81ae-5d08-8a2d-0c6bb6bee4b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381381} {:answer/id #uuid "fc99431b-bcd1-55b5-84fd-f14b97a88c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381382} {:answer/id #uuid "766be54f-1975-5937-af45-f1af61a2dd65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381383} {:answer/id #uuid "c38ade46-ad25-5052-a8f1-78b5162567a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381384} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe4f045-8304-53fa-9423-89bc381460b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fe4f045-8304-53fa-9423-89bc381460b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381385} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d185b6e-b0a4-5a37-b02c-812c195b6ece" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e5a419a-5284-52a1-8d61-0b690fdca30e" :question/math-academy-id "q-6825" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=3t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}+1},\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $a$ | $-8$ | $-5$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $4$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $b$ | $5$ | $10$ | $5$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $x=3t-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=3(-3)-2=-9-2=-11.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-2$ into $y=\\frac{10}{{t}^{2}+1}.$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{10}{(-2)^{2}+1}=\\frac{10}{5}=2.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $x=3t-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\na=3(3)-2=9-2=7.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=-11+2+7=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef42b53-c947-565c-92f4-0805416a3f83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 5,\\,-9\\le y\\le -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301710} {:answer/id #uuid "d29e53c2-1b0f-5cc4-97e9-ccec68455ed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6\\le x\\le 0,\\,1\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301711} {:answer/id #uuid "72f9f89e-712b-5ab7-a5c4-cc178ff9b7af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le 13,\\,3\\le y\\le 7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301712} {:answer/id #uuid "31684924-c9a6-510c-9776-e96f4ceef062" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0\\le x\\le 5,\\,-7\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301713} {:answer/id #uuid "969c2915-2689-5624-bfb9-1ac9afca9aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969c2915-2689-5624-bfb9-1ac9afca9aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\le x\\le 6,\\,-13\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301714} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea0f38ba-c575-54aa-a792-f2d49224cee9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2602fecb-27f9-522e-9ca3-fc225206d948" :question/math-academy-id "q-70523" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ndetermine the ranges of $x$ and $y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -1+5 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5\\le 1+5 \\\\ 4 & \\le x\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $4\\le x\\le 6.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -6 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 6 \\\\ -6-7 & \\le 6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -7\\le 6-7 \\\\ -13 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-13\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $4\\le x\\le 6$ and $-13\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152459ab-3a1a-5b9e-b9ac-5cd22ca380f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381388} {:answer/id #uuid "174dec79-2f09-50eb-bf28-1001b4cc6e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381389} {:answer/id #uuid "f0efdf3e-ff3b-5ebc-8eb2-53779c7320ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 6,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381390} {:answer/id #uuid "1de52e1a-54f1-5c02-a6fe-d5494cb210ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le y\\le 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381391} {:answer/id #uuid "33456b5b-8350-51e8-934a-40597148321b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 4,\\,-1\\le y\\le 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "174dec79-2f09-50eb-bf28-1001b4cc6e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-5\\le y\\le -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381389} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dfed808-fac2-5660-822b-bc2b49f54b6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10cc8d42-b8f4-514b-90c8-1f7d2d032156" :question/math-academy-id "q-73626" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\ge -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\text{ }\\ge -2 \\\\ -2 & \\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ 1-2 & \\le 1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1+2 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2-3 & \\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3\\le 2-3 \\\\ -5 & \\le y\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-5\\le y\\le -1.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $-5\\le y\\le -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cddc940d-2185-569c-a62b-b507e95d3c35" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation Defined in Terms of Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f96514d7-3065-5303-a087-6259f0105fd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381395} {:answer/id #uuid "d3587613-f27b-54df-9253-fb35d7062213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381396} {:answer/id #uuid "5c44c3fa-6b0c-595a-98a3-0239751bfeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le y\\le 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381397} {:answer/id #uuid "6496dbc4-3eae-51b0-8e01-7e04ea0134e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381398} {:answer/id #uuid "29800e71-5b65-5c06-af6c-c660797a4d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3587613-f27b-54df-9253-fb35d7062213" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381396} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d53d166-17b4-5aea-bd79-273800de1c38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381394}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba2cff68-3e52-52c7-b34d-7c5994e37231" :question/math-academy-id "q-73632" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -2 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\le 2 \\\\ -2+1 & \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1\\le 2+1 \\\\ -1 & \\le x\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $-1\\le x\\le 3.$\n\n- To find the range of $y,$ notice that for $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ we have $-1\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1.$ Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\le \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & \\le \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\le \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & \\le y\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can conclude that $1\\le y\\le 2.$\n\nTherefore, the ranges are $-1\\le x\\le 3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$"}]}
